WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:00.943
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:00.943 --> 00:00:02.770
Happy New Year, happy 2021.
(gentle music)

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:04.610
Sitting here in Washington, DC,

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.840
right outside of what is
increasingly becoming a war zone,

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.450
because this week there is a, hopefully,

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:15.270
peaceful transfer of power
from one leader to another.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.950
Anyway, let's talk about journalism.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.620
I sort of stumbled into journalism.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.630
At first I wanted to be like
a cinematographer, filmmaker.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.250
Then I wanted to be a
diplomat and I wanted to

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:26.380
join the Foreign Service
for the United States.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.700
Then I learned that I didn't
wanna do either of those things

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.280
and that I could actually
combine ingredients from both

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.650
to do this thing called journalism,

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.860
communicating kind of complex topics

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.130
of the day to an audience.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:40.490
I didn't go to journalism school,

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.100
I didn't formally study this,

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.950
but I ended up becoming what
most would call a journalist.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:47.020
Over the years I've learned

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.430
a few things about journalism

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.090
that I wanna share with you.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.277
- [Narrator] I assign a reporter to get

00:00:52.277 --> 00:00:54.120
all the important facts
and to write the story.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.460
I also assign a news
photographer to take pictures.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.010
The recorder and photographer
will work together as a team.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.800
Now the reporter has all the facts.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.570
He's ready to write his story.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.400
He puts the most important facts
in the first few sentences.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.320
Then he adds the interesting details.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.690
- So let's make a list here.
(bright music)

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:11.960
Number 1: "Objectivity is a myth."

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.140
There's no such thing as objectivity.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.130
I often find myself in these conversations

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:16.997
where people are just like,

00:01:16.997 --> 00:01:20.400
"If there was just
unbiased, objective news,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.910
all of our problems in the
media would just go away."

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:27.330
That's a really attractive
idea, but the more I do this,

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:31.160
the more I realize that
objectivity isn't a thing,

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:31.993
it's a theory.

00:01:31.993 --> 00:01:34.450
It's not a real thing
you can ever achieve.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.360
Every article you read,
every video you watch,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.410
every photo you see was
produced by a human being

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.080
who went out and gathered that information

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.150
and then decided what
facts to put down on paper.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:49.630
They chose to frame the
issue a certain way,

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.690
to leave out some stuff
and to include other stuff.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:53.750
There were choices there.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:55.440
And those choices were the product

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.590
of a human and their biases.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.300
Even a photo journalist,

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.010
someone who takes pictures for newspapers,

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:04.550
presumably the least bias type
of journalism there can be,

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:06.730
is still totally subject to bias.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:09.440
The way that photo is cropped and framed,

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.860
and who they decided to take
a photo of, and at what time,

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.820
and how they decided to expose the frame,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.060
all of those are choices.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:17.900
Objectivity is a myth.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.700
But what isn't a myth, and what's real?

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.210
And I think what people
are sort of getting at

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.680
when they talk about
objectivity, is fairness.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.000
Being fair to your topic, or your subject,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.670
or the other side of the argument

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:28.920
that you're making in your article.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:29.910
My old boss Ezra Klein
(gentle music)

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.330
used to talk about this
concept as generosity.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.990
We, as journalists go out and gather

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.250
a bunch of information
and facts about a thing,

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.040
and then we sort of land somewhere

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.720
on what we think about that topic.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.360
And our journalism, our story,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.200
is gonna reflect that decision.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.960
But what you can do as a fair journalist

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.170
is you could be generous
to the other side.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.580
You can present the best version

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.010
of the other side's argument.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.610
That to me is what healthy journalism has,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.840
is generosity to the other side.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.370
But objectivity, it just doesn't exist.

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:02.390
I experienced this in the
first season of "Borders"

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.430
when I was in Japan doing
that North Korea bubble story.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.870
I clearly had a position in that story,

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:12.640
but at the same time, I wanted
to give the best version,

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.130
the most generous version, of
the North Korean viewpoint.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.290
And I tried to split that balance

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:21.460
while also being clear and
assertive about the facts.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.910
That's a difficult balance,
but when it's done right,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.370
it makes for better journalism.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.120
Number 2: "A lot of journalists

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.320
write for their peers and
not for their audience."

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.380
This is another really
(gentle music)

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.210
useful thing that I learned
from Ezra Working at "Vox."

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.070
A lot of journalism is hard to understand,

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:37.010
that's just a fact.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.350
Like even now when I read
the "Wall Street Journal"

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.160
or the "Washington Post"
or the "New York Times,"

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.650
I have a hard time getting
around some of the language

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.590
and some of the things that are happening,

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.110
especially for topics
that I'm sort of new to.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.320
I've always felt this.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.607
And it's always sort of been
frustrating, 'cause I'm like,

00:03:51.607 --> 00:03:53.920
"Aren't you guys writing
so that I understand?

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.240
Like, I'm an educated
consumer of this news

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.110
and yet I'm having a
hard time understanding."

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:59.160
Like, that's a problem.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.350
A lot of journalism is
written for insiders.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.010
People who are already kind
of familiar with the topic.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:05.843
As a journalist,

00:04:05.843 --> 00:04:07.810
when you're writing about
something that's kind of complex,

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:09.580
you start to sort of get in your head.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.420
You're like, "Man, a lot of
people are gonna see this.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.300
And a lot of really smart
people are gonna see this.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.700
So if I use really simple language,

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.130
what are my peers gonna think?

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:18.850
What are my journalists
friends down the road

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:20.510
at the "Washington Post"
or the "New York Times"

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:22.180
gonna think about this article

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.090
if I don't use all the fancy jargon?"

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.600
If I'm explaining the
financial crisis in Venezuela,

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.757
it's a lot more tempting
to write something like,

00:04:28.757 --> 00:04:31.770
"Hyperinflation has led to
the complete devaluation

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.670
of the local currencies
spurring a collapse in wages,"

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.160
which shows my peers that
I'm actually really smart.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:38.110
I have a Master's degree

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:40.030
in International Relations and Economics.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.340
But what about my audience

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.770
who knows nothing about hyperinflation?

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:47.490
They're left feeling left
out and no more informed

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.060
about the financial crisis in Venezuela.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.980
Instead, what I should do
is write something like,

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.000
quote, "Situation has
gotten so bad in Venezuela

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.240
that the money is worth
a thousand times less

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:00.380
than it was last year,

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.850
making those who were once really rich

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.670
now struggling to find their next meal."

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:05.790
It's still accurate.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:06.800
It's still smart.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.110
And it's still an accurate assertion

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:10.790
about what's happening in Venezuela.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.150
I'm just not using any of the jargon.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.560
If my peers judge me for that, so be it.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.550
At least my audience will understand.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.760
Number 3: "Journalism has a lot

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.000
of very old customs or traditions."

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.150
There are a lot of old practices
and habits in journalism

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:29.290
that feel like gospel to a
lot of people in journalism.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:31.300
One of the big ones that I always run into

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.740
is that in sort of traditional
conventional journalism

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.650
we don't say the word "I."
(light music)

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.320
The journalist is a fly on the wall,

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.420
we're not there to insert ourselves,

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:43.040
our personal character into the story,

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:44.840
therefore we don't say the word, "I."

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.910
And I sort of understand
the thinking behind that

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:48.250
even if I don't practice it.

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.240
The other convention or custom,

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.930
mainly in like documentary
and video journalism,

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.930
is that we're not here to make
things look too beautiful.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.040
Beautiful B-roll, evocative music,

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.070
beautiful motion graphics,

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:03.150
those are for like the brands
and the corporate people.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.347
Here in journalism we care about

00:06:05.347 --> 00:06:06.520
"The story".
(drums boom)

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.910
And all the fancy stuff is just
fluff, not necessary at all.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.460
I disagree with both of those.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:14.670
I disagree with the aversion to using "I,"

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:17.240
and I disagree with the aversion to using

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.000
beautiful imagery and B-roll and music.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.670
Luckily, when I came into journalism,

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.370
I came into an organization
that was very eager

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:28.357
to disrupt this old set
of customs in journalism.

00:06:28.357 --> 00:06:31.080
"Vox" really encouraged me to experiment.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.590
Thank goodness they did.

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:35.440
I was able to start a
series that was all about

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.600
showing beautiful visuals
of places around the world,

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.010
and it had music and
it had motion graphics.

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:44.790
And of course, it had a lot of
turning the camera on myself

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:46.410
and talking to the camera.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.440
I was a character in the story,

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.550
and I would go to these conferences,

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:51.960
these journalism conferences,

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.750
and I would always get
sort of interrogated

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:55.657
by these legacy news people who were like,

00:06:55.657 --> 00:06:57.820
"Can you really consider this journalism?"

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:00.610
And my response was always, "I don't know.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:02.300
Like, you can decide,

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.350
I'm not here to chase
the label of journalism.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:06.680
I'm here to tell stories about people

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:09.250
and to bring big audiences
to those stories."

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:11.790
The reality is a lot of these customs

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:14.490
are sort of arbitrary
and they were developed

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:18.530
at a different time when
information was shared differently.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:20.790
Let me caveat just one big thing here

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.960
by saying that a lot of these norms,

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.440
and a lot of these customs
and ethics within journalism,

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.640
are actually really useful.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:31.170
The idea of staying honest and fact-based,

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:34.310
and accurate, and making
sure that your assertions

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.670
actually have some evidence to them,

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:37.860
I am all for that.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.870
I love that there is a
very strong tradition

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:43.207
and burden of evidence
within journalism that says,

00:07:43.207 --> 00:07:45.330
"If you can't prove it, it's not real,"

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:47.860
especially because a lot of those customs,

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:50.410
which I believe are the
really important customs

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:53.920
of factual information, are
sort of going out the window.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:55.070
And if you need proof of that,

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:58.170
just go five miles away
from me to the Capitol,

00:07:58.170 --> 00:07:59.070
like a week ago,

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:02.160
and see how that was covered
by journalists on both sides.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.657
When it comes to the formats, the,

00:08:04.657 --> 00:08:06.890
"Oh, that guy turned
the camera on himself"

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:08.500
or, "Did a bunch of drone shots

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:09.880
and therefore it's not journalism."

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:12.620
I'm here to tell stories that have facts,

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:16.680
and data, and characters
and help inform people.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:18.980
If you decide to call
that journalism or not,

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:19.813
I don't really care.

00:08:19.813 --> 00:08:21.130
It's how I do it.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.950
But I guess the lesson here is that

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:24.430
if you're going into journalism,

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:27.610
those are really serious intense customs

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:29.700
that you will come in contact with.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:33.057
Which gets me to my next
lesson which is number 4:

00:08:33.057 --> 00:08:35.700
"Journalism school isn't
always the best way

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:36.950
to get into journalism."

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:38.950
Now, let me just pause for a second

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:40.580
and make it clear that I am not saying

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:42.020
don't go to journalism school.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.560
Most of the people who
have taught me what I know

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:46.930
about the craft of journalism
went to journalism school

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:48.390
and I am deeply grateful.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.740
Thank you, Joss Fong and others

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:52.230
for teaching me how to do journalism

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:53.890
when I frankly didn't
know what I was doing

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:55.980
when I walked in the door,
the first day at "Vox".

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:57.380
But, let me make it clear
(bright music)

00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:00.090
that going to journalism
school is not the only

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:03.220
or even the best way
to get into journalism.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:06.020
Man, I've said journalism,
the word journalism,

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:07.660
so many times in this video

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.490
it's starting to get
to the point where like

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:12.680
the word feels weird to say,
because I've said it so much.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:15.310
So anyway, my critique
of journalism school

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:17.710
is the same critique
I have of film school,

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:20.060
which is something I started and stopped

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:21.120
after about a year and a half.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:22.790
The critique for both of these is that

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:26.070
the institution spends so
much time focusing on theory,

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:30.400
tradition, history, critique
of the old style of doing it,

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.150
the old way of doing filmmaking,

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:33.940
or the old way of doing journalism,

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.350
that they don't give
the students much chance

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:38.780
to actually experiment and play around

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:41.560
with like, the new versions of
how this thing is being done.

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:44.940
We spend so much time looking
back and talking to faculty

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:47.810
who sort of had their heyday
in, like, the old days,

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:48.980
that they don't spend a lot of time

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:51.420
doing the type of journalism
that happens today,

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.790
which is under very
different circumstances

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:56.070
economically, politically, et cetera.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:57.960
So, my big critique is that

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.770
there isn't enough, in the trenches,

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:01.060
experience with journalism,

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.000
and that you ended up getting a sort of

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.910
warped version of what
you're gonna be doing,

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:08.460
and a lot of fixed ideas that
are hard to, sort of shift,

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.670
when you actually get
out into the real world.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:11.950
Now, there are a lot of journalism schools

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:14.720
who are doing really great
work in this department

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:16.750
and letting their students
see what it's really like.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:19.070
I go and speak to a lot of
these journalism schools,

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:20.670
whether it's the University of Oregon,

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.970
or Stanford, or Yale or whatever it is.

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:25.140
When I speak to students at these schools

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.620
I see an appetite from
faculty to give these students

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:32.320
perspective on what
journalism looks like in 2021.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:35.060
What it looks like in this
new age of digital journalism,

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.930
and what are the different versions

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:39.030
of journalism that one can go into?

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:40.820
The big strength of journalism school

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:44.020
is that they teach you the
importance of reporting,

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:45.730
I mean, going out and gathering facts,

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:48.730
of fairness, of facts,
of accuracy, of evidence,

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:50.880
the heart of journalism you
learn in journalism school.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:53.330
Absolutely. That is totally irrefutable.

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:54.170
I believe in that,

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:57.320
but I believe that you
get a lot of that fluff

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:59.530
on top of the customs and,

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:00.960
how you're supposed to do journalism,

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:01.960
and what is and what isn't.

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:04.730
And those fixed ideas,
if they root too deeply

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:07.120
can hinder you once you
get out into the field.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.330
Go there, learn, like, go for it,

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:11.210
but make sure you don't get too entrenched

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:13.510
on what journalism is and isn't.

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:14.927
The next lesson is that:

00:11:14.927 --> 00:11:18.440
"Journalism is economic
in its very nature."

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.250
It has to be, it is a business.

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:21.660
Just watched "Little Women"
(bright music)

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:23.550
the new one with Hermione,
it was really good.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:25.140
I watched "Little Women"
a bunch growing up

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.120
but I'd never really bought it.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:27.860
And then I watched this one
and it totally made sense.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:28.693
And I loved it.

00:11:28.693 --> 00:11:31.637
Anyway, there's this amazing
line in there where she says,

00:11:31.637 --> 00:11:34.140
- "So don't sit there
and tell me that marriage

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:36.130
isn't an economic
proposition because it is."

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:38.880
- Journalism is an economic proposition,

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:40.830
meaning if you're starting
a journalism outlet

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.390
in the United States, you
are doing so as a business.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.580
You need to keep your lights
on and your doors open

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:48.480
by selling something,
whether it's subscriptions,

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.700
or whether it's ad's or whether
it's some other new version

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.710
of economic value that you can
create with your journalism.

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:55.660
You have to do that.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:59.180
And with that comes a certain set of

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:03.390
expectations and incentives
that aren't necessarily aligned

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:06.230
with the incentives of
telling really good stories.

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:07.063
And now of course,

00:12:07.063 --> 00:12:09.167
I've ranted about this in
a previous video called,

00:12:09.167 --> 00:12:10.400
"Why I hate the news."

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:14.260
I'm not gonna go into the
depths of my thoughts on this.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.010
If you want to go watch
that video, you should.

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:17.270
But, whenever I go to speak

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:18.860
at a journalism conference in Europe,

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:22.150
I always chat with them about
the economics of journalism

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:24.640
in Switzerland or the
Netherlands or whatever.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.260
And in Europe they've
really realized that like,

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:29.550
private media can get out of control

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:31.240
and can lead to some really bad outcomes.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:33.270
And so, they really try to buffer

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:36.880
the media machine from private interests,

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:38.300
and they do so, and a pretty good job,

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:39.570
there's still private media in Europe.

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:40.760
But here in the United States,

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:42.730
private media is the name of the game.

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:45.950
And the effects of these
incentives on journalism

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:47.490
are very apparent.

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:50.810
We are incentivized to chase
volume, to chasing numbers,

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:52.690
to chase like a little bit of

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:54.050
what's gonna get people to click?

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:58.020
It's hard to compete when
the information landscape

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:00.240
incentivizes us to be in that environment.

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:03.640
Money usually comes from
attention and advertising

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:06.400
not necessarily subscribers who

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:09.470
pay a monthly fee for your newspaper.

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:11.680
And attention and traffic

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:15.250
are not always conducive
to telling the best story

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:16.870
or doing the best journalism,

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:17.920
which is a real travesty.

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:20.070
The best thing that took the most time,

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:23.330
the biggest article that
has the most information,

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:27.730
is not actually the most valued
among the attention economy.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:29.850
But, anyway, I'm not here to lament

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:31.040
human psychology.
(muted cheers)

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:33.890
I do offer two pieces of hope to this

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:36.310
capitalistic incentive
of the media quandary.

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:37.720
Number one is that

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:39.780
there are still really great outlets

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.030
that publish really in-depth media

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:44.410
and that you just have to pay money to.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:47.770
And by doing so you
shield the need for them

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:49.830
to have to just chase pure volume.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:51.460
One of my favorite outlets of all time

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:53.120
and where I get most of my news

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:54.920
and my ways of thinking about the world,

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.900
is a British weekly magazine
called "The Economist."

00:13:57.900 --> 00:13:58.840
And I pay for it.

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:02.040
I like to consume media
that I know was crafted

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:05.150
by the incentives of serving
me, the paying customer,

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:07.400
as opposed to serving a different master,

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:10.040
which is an advertiser
who will keep them afloat.

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:11.710
There's still a lot of amazing media

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:14.700
that happens through advertiser
revenue, but just as a fact,

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:16.330
most media had to pivot to that,

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:20.060
but, more and more, those
incentives are pushing media

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:23.720
into more sensationalized,
polarized places.

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:24.780
Okay, my second
(bright music)

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:26.900
piece of hope here is that

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:29.060
when I joined "Vox", we were
just sort of taking off.

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:31.070
We had less than 100,000
subscribers on YouTube.

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:32.350
It was like the early days.

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:35.300
And there was this hypothesis
among the whole organization,

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:36.800
which was like a few dozen people,

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:40.080
that if we could tell really good stories,

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:42.930
and explain really
complicated things to people,

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.220
that we would actually win the views game,

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:47.160
like, people would actually come to us

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:48.770
instead of the funny cat video,

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:50.830
because they actually want to understand.

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:53.400
People actually want to
engage with good information.

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:56.470
And it turns out that that
hypothesis was totally correct.

00:14:56.470 --> 00:14:59.520
The most popular videos
I ever made at "Vox",

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:03.910
one, a six minute explainer of
a complex Middle Eastern war,

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:06.750
really complicated, but got
hundreds of millions of views.

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:08.340
And another really popular video I made

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:10.650
was a 10 minute video explaining

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:14.630
the housing policy crisis
and tax law in Hong Kong.

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:17.380
Like, some of those boring
but important information

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:18.240
you could ever think of.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:21.450
But, because they were presented in a way

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:23.070
that people could access,

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:24.770
it actually made them really popular.

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:27.020
And people said, "Yes,
I want to understand

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:28.610
what's going on with the Syrian civil war,

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:29.818
because everyone's talking about it

00:15:29.818 --> 00:15:30.651
and I don't understand it.

00:15:30.651 --> 00:15:31.930
I will sit through this six minute video."

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:33.650
And that video by the way, took off,

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:35.520
not on YouTube, but on Facebook.

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.910
Like, the place where people's attention

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.330
is apparently the lowest and
where people are just there

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:42.300
to click on whatever's sensational.

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:43.710
That's a problem, that's happening,

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:47.330
but you can still get people
to watch really in-depth stuff

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:49.180
if it's well told and well presented.

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:52.480
So that, that's a hopeful
thing to my little lament

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:54.600
about the economics of journalism.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:55.790
Man, I'm getting deep right now.

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:58.040
I'm getting like deep into
my theories of journalism.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:15:59.263
Yeah. Yeah.

00:15:59.263 --> 00:16:00.120
This is good. This is good.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:01.190
Let's get onto the next lesson.

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:05.300
Number 6: "Good writing
is rare and beautiful."

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:06.910
What I've learned in journalism is that

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:08.140
the difference between someone

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:09.920
really connecting with a story

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:13.010
and not connecting with a
story, is just a few words.

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:15.410
Good writing, whether it's for a video

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:19.520
or a text-based article, it
can bring you into a story.

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:22.600
It can illuminate a topic,
it can humanize a character.

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:23.970
It's really important.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:27.220
I wasn't very good at writing
when I started at "Vox".

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:29.930
And over the years I had some
really good story editors

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:31.940
who would look at my writing and help me

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:33.910
reshape it and simplify it,

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:36.600
and make it more concise
and make it clearer.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:39.410
I am grateful for the people
who taught me how to write,

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:41.830
because, a lot of the videos
you see on this channel,

00:16:41.830 --> 00:16:45.200
yeah, there is beautiful maps,
and there's fancy footage,

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:47.040
and there's lots of
drone shots and whatever.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:49.150
But at the end of the
day, behind all of that,

00:16:49.150 --> 00:16:51.450
the reason why you're interested
in watching those videos

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:54.130
is because I put a major
priority on writing.

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:56.840
I spent so much time choosing my words

00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:58.940
so that every story I tell
(bright music)

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:01.540
sinks in on the other
side when you're listening

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:03.380
and that's because good journalism

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:05.510
is good writing at the end of the day.

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:07.050
And it's incredibly important.

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.500
If you want to get better at
writing, I have a book for you.

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.140
I'm gonna put the link in the description.

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:13.100
If I forget to do that,
call me out in the comments

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:14.490
because I don't wanna forget

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:15.960
because I wanna tell you about this book.

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:17.130
I mean, I could just say it right now

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:18.160
and then you can go find it.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:21.030
But oh, but if I put it in
this link in the description

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:22.530
I could make it an affiliate link,

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:25.330
and then I could get money
every time you click it.

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:28.990
Man, the incentives of
the 21st century economy

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:30.950
are just playing on my mind right now.

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:31.880
Oh gosh.

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:35.840
Okay. The last lesson here,
"Good journalism is important."

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:36.673
Now hear me out on this.
(bright electronic music)

00:17:36.673 --> 00:17:37.860
This might sound trite, but,

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:40.770
I've learned over the years
that journalism is hard.

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:43.040
To go out and gather information

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:44.350
that doesn't exist in the world

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:48.560
and to communicate it to the
rest of the world is hard.

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.680
Our country, these past few months,

00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:52.750
has shown me just how important

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:55.920
and how sort of delicate
good journalism can be.

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:59.420
Facts don't matter for a
lot of people these days.

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:00.253
And before you know it,

00:18:00.253 --> 00:18:02.850
you can have a person in power

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:05.970
creating fake stories about an election,

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:07.690
and you can have a news outlet

00:18:07.690 --> 00:18:09.070
that claims to be doing journalism,

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:10.830
jump on board with that narrative

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:13.600
and make up facts to support it.

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:14.490
That's scary!

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:17.080
Good journalism, the stuff
where people are going out

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:19.700
and actually scrutinizing evidence

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:23.470
and presenting robust
arguments to an audience

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:25.630
that is scrupulous back towards them,

00:18:25.630 --> 00:18:27.930
and there's a discussion, a discourse,

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:29.550
that's rare and special.

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:32.260
Here in the United States we
call it the "Fourth Estate",

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:34.690
this institution of free press

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:36.730
that keeps our leaders honest,

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:39.410
keeps our corporations honest,

00:18:39.410 --> 00:18:43.360
keeps our society together and honest.

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:45.570
There will always be
people and institutions

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:47.380
whose sole purpose is to gain.

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:51.680
Journalism offers a
spotlight or a watchdog,

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:54.860
on those actors on those institutions.

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:58.350
And because of that, it's
really, really important.

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:01.600
I am very, very grateful
for those journalists,

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:04.130
especially right now,
this time in history,

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:07.850
where covering the facts
is so freaking important

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:10.630
for keeping our society
peaceful and together,

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:12.400
and honest and working.

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:13.600
I just, I'm grateful

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:15.080
for the good journalism that's happening,

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:16.270
the people out there hustling.

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:18.710
And I lament the hostility

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:20.900
that has been shown towards those people

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:25.670
and I hope we can move on and
get back to facts and truth.

